import { JsonReaderCopy } from "../model"

pub fn copy -> JsonReaderCopy = JsonReaderCopy {
  summary: "Convert a typed value such as settings to Json before writing text. Pass that Json to another operation, or use stringify when a string is needed.",
  taskTitle: "Build an intermediate JSON value from settings",
  purpose: "Separate converting settings from writing text. First build Json from Settings containing name and retries, then display it as a string. The reading half also separates parsing JSON from checking Settings.",
  typescriptBefore: "TypeScript encodeSettings constructs an output object with name and retries, and its caller uses JSON.stringify to write text. decodeSettings checks unknown data and returns ok: true with value or ok: false with reason. The JSON.parse input is a fixed, valid literal in this program.",
  benefit: "encodeJson marks the step from an application value to a JSON value. You do not need to produce text yet, so you can pass the result to operations that take Json. Its result type differs from stringify, which produces text.",
  setup: "json is the alias for std/json. struct Settings defines a named type with name: String and retries: Int. deriving JsonEncode, JsonDecode supplies conversion to JSON and checking from JSON for that type. encodeJson and decodeJson are available from std/prelude, so the example does not import them individually.",
  after: [
    "let encodeSettings: json.Encoder<Settings> = encodeJson stores encodeJson, selected for Settings, under the name encodeSettings. Encoder<Settings> after the colon is an alias for Settings -> Json; -> describes the function's input and output types. No settings value is converted on this line yet.",
    "Settings { name: \"Mio\", retries: 2 } constructs a settings value. Pass it after encodeSettings to produce Json. Function arguments follow the name, separated by spaces. Parentheses group the inner call, and passing its result to json.stringify then produces the String to display.",
    "let decodeSettings: json.Decoder<Settings> = decodeJson stores the checking function in the reverse direction. Decoder<Settings> means Json -> Either<DecodeError, Settings>. Either holds one of two outcomes: a failure reason in Left or successful Settings in Right.",
    "fn readParsed source: String -> String is an ordinary function taking text, with its return type after ->. match json.parse source checks whether the text could be read. For Right value, the next match checks decodeSettings value. Right settings -> joins settings.name to a message with +. Left _ -> returns a failure message without the detail; _ means that detail is unused.",
    "pub effect fn main is the output entry point. Unit means no useful return value; with Console fails ConsoleError declares console use and possible output failure. do sequences println calls, and _ <- ignores the first print result. JSON text and Ready: Mio appear on separate lines. Inside a string, \\\" writes a JSON double quote.",
  ],
  details: [
    "A in encodeJson<A> is the input type. where JsonEncode<A> requires JSON conversion support for that type; deriving JsonEncode supplies it for Settings here. This named Settings writes fields in declaration order, and String and Int also need conversion support.",
    "encodeJson does not return String. If you only need text rather than an intermediate Json, json.encodeString combines conversion and writing. encodeJson does not save files or make network requests.",
    "Converting settings to JSON does not validate application-specific business rules. Check separately if names must be nonempty or retry counts nonnegative. The comparison shows output for the example's strings and small integers.",
    "If TypeScript receives external JSON text instead of the fixed literal, JSON.parse also needs failure handling such as try/catch. Seseragi readParsed includes a failure branch for both parsing and checking the requested type.",
  ],
}
